From f55b6e330caef6c22038126146ca2618b948558f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 03:15:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/24] Follow the room light with the built-in display's
 brightness

macOS ships automatic brightness on for the built-in display of the
MacBook Pro 14" M4, and its ambient light sensor (aop-als) already
reports the illuminance on Linux. Stock Omarchy has no automatic
brightness yet (omacom/omarchy#8025 is open), so nothing uses it.

omarchy-mac-brightness-auto reads the sensor once a second, looks the
room up in a per-model curve under /usr/share/omarchy-mac/brightness
(lux to nits, from macOS's own curve for this panel) and moves the
backlight there in macOS's 1.94 s ramp. A small change in the light is
left alone. A level the user sets by hand scales the curve for every
room, as macOS scales its curve. A panel that is off is not woken, a
Mac with no table or no sensor is left alone, and the service steps
aside while Omarchy's own omarchy-brightness-auto runs, so the two never
drive the panel at once. "omarchy-mac-brightness-auto off" stops it;
"on" resumes. A user unit runs it with the graphical session.
